# Ctrl-Z on a *foreground* block containing a `cmd` (`{ { cmd sleep N } }`), then `bg`,
# then killing the process with `host:signal`, must leave the `cmd` job reported as
# `Terminated` -- same as `pty_ctrl_z_bg_then_signal_shows_terminated.crush`, but nested.
#
# This shape pauses only the innermost statement's job (the one `current_job()` finds),
# and no `Job::eval` ever registers it as a background job, so "this job left the
# foreground" can't be tied to that registration -- it's recorded by
# `GlobalState::pause` itself (see `JobData::backgrounded`). Found while enumerating
# every way a job can stop holding the foreground.
#
# The paused job's id is looked up (highest-id `Paused` row) rather than hardcoded, since
# it depends on how many intermediate block jobs exist. Pid lookup and settle delays
# follow `pty_terminate_and_signal_external_job.crush` (see its header).
#
# KNOWN GAP, not converted to the counted-prompt/`pty_send_control` approach the other
# 7 Ctrl-Z/Ctrl-C pty tests now use (see pty_test_helpers.crush): tried directly,
# converted this exact way, and found it genuinely unreliable here specifically --
# roughly 2 in 3 runs failed with no job ever reaching `Paused` at all, even though the
# *simpler*, non-nested Ctrl-Z cases (a bare `sleep`, a bare `cmd sleep`) convert
# cleanly and have run clean well over 100 times each, including under artificial CPU
# load, with the exact same technique. Three levels of nested block/closure dispatch
# before the innermost `cmd` job exists appears to measurably widen the real window
# `pty_ctrl_z_suspends_external_job.crush`'s own header already documents (Ctrl-Z
# racing the fork/exec/terminal-handoff) -- confirmed the race is real and not just an
# artifact of the test by adding targeted tracing, which consistently made it stop
# reproducing entirely (added latency on the signal-handling thread gives the nested
# dispatch enough extra time to finish first) -- the exact signature of a genuine,
# very narrow timing race, not a logic bug in this file's own retry helper. Left as a
# fixed delay pending further investigation, same as
# `pty_fg_then_signal_leaves_no_terminated_row.crush`'s own documented gap.
$crush_bin := $(crush:env["CRUSH_BIN"])
$t := $(pty:create $crush_bin)
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 300ms

$t:write "{ { cmd sleep 7432 } }\n"
$t:wait_for "sleep"
sleep 500ms
$t:write $t:control:z
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms
$t:write "$bgid := ($(jobs | where {($status == \"Paused\")} | select id | sort id reverse=$true))[0]:id\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms
$t:write "bg $bgid\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms

$t:write "$my_pid := $(crush:pid)\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 300ms
$t:write "$pid := ($(host:procs | where {(($name =~ ^(.*sleep)) and ($ppid == $my_pid))} | select pid | sort pid reverse=$true))[0]:pid\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 1000ms
$t:write "host:signal $pid signal=SIGKILL\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms
$t:write "$n := $(jobs | where {($status == \"Terminated\")} | count)\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms
$t:write "echo $(\"NESTED_Z_CHECK_{}\":format $n)\n"
$t:wait_for "NESTED_Z_CHECK_1"

$t:close
echo "ok"
